section.para:
			Annotate a document, read the layer, then produce a redacted copy in which
			only detected spans are replaced. Length is preserved: every character
			inside a mention becomes the mask character, and everything outside stays
			unchanged. PiiUsageExampleTest asserts the behavior shown
			here.

String text =
    "Contact jane@example.com, call (555) 123-4567, or charge card 4111 1111 1111 1111.";

Document document = new PiiAnnotator(new CursorPiiExtractor())
    .annotate(Document.of(text));

List<Annotation<PiiMention>> mentions = document.get(PiiAnnotator.PII);
// email, phone, card: exact spans, types, and normalized forms

String masked = Masker.mask(document, PiiAnnotator.PII, '*');
// "Contact ****************, call **************, or charge card *******************."

			Masking accepts positional layers only. A document-scoped layer, such as
			a language identifier, has no spans and is rejected with
			IllegalArgumentException. Each policy reads the original
			text. When spans overlap, a character remains masked if either span
			redacts it. Layer order does not affect the result when using one policy:

LayerKey<String> left = LayerKey.of("left", String.class);
LayerKey<String> right = LayerKey.of("right", String.class);
Document overlaps = Document.of("123456")
    .with(left, List.of(new Annotation<>(new Span(0, 4), "1234")))
    .with(right, List.of(new Annotation<>(new Span(2, 6), "3456")));

String masked = Masker.mask(overlaps, List.of(right, left),
    MaskPolicy.of('*').keepingTrailing(2));
// "****56" with either layer order

			SIN and IMEI labels are ASCII case-insensitive and must not continue a
			Unicode letter or digit. Whitespace and :, # or
			= may separate labels from values; SIN also permits
			.. Both detectors check format and checksum, not assignment
			to a person or device. This example masks only the numeric spans:

PiiExtractor labeled = new CompositePiiExtractor(PiiPacks.caIdentity(), PiiPacks.device());
Document identifiers = new PiiAnnotator(labeled)
    .annotate(Document.of("SIN: 046 454 286; IMEI: 490154203237518."));
String masked = Masker.mask(identifiers, PiiAnnotator.PII, '*');
// "SIN: ***********; IMEI: ***************."

			This synthetic routing number illustrates extraction and masking without
			using a bank-directory entry:

Document payment = new PiiAnnotator(PiiPacks.payment())
    .annotate(Document.of("Routing 001234561."));
String masked = Masker.mask(payment, PiiAnnotator.PII, '*');
// "Routing *********."

CompositePiiExtractor runs several extractors over one text and merges the result. PiiPacks provides payment(), contact(), network(), secrets(), crypto(), usIdentity(), euIdentity(), caIdentity(), device(), and allStructured() for all supported structured types. The leftmost candidate wins an overlap, then the longest, then the type with the lower PiiTypePriority rank. Delegate order breaks equal ranks. Nested composites contribute their individual extractors before overlaps are resolved, in depth-first, left-to-right order.
String text = "Deploy from 10.0.0.7 using AKIAIOSFODNN7EXAMPLE, ask jane@example.com.";
List<PiiMention> mentions = PiiPacks.allStructured().extract(text); // ipv4, aws-access-key, email
PiiExtractor keysOnly = PiiPacks.secrets(); PiiExtractor custom = new CompositePiiExtractor( new CursorPiiExtractor(), new NetworkPiiExtractor());

			Grouping the same ordered extractors into nested composites does not
			change their result. In this example, selecting the leftmost span rejects
			the middle candidate but retains the final person mention:

PiiExtractor first = text -> List.of(new PiiMention(new Span(0, 3), "person", "Amy"));
PiiExtractor middle = text -> List.of(new PiiMention(new Span(1, 5), "custom", "my B"));
PiiExtractor last = text -> List.of(new PiiMention(new Span(4, 7), "person", "Bob"));
PiiExtractor combined = new CompositePiiExtractor(first,
    new CompositePiiExtractor(middle, last));
Document people = new PiiAnnotator(combined).annotate(Document.of("Amy Bob"));
String masked = Masker.mask(people, PiiAnnotator.PII, '*');
// "*** ***"

			Pseudonymizer replaces mentions with numbered labels. Mentions
			with the same type and normalized value use the same label within a text.
			PiiRewrite provides the rewritten text, label locations and
			offset mapping.

String text = "Contact jane@example.com; jane@example.com replied to bob@example.com.";

PiiRewrite rewrite = new Pseudonymizer()
    .rewrite(text, new CursorPiiExtractor().extract(text));
// "Contact EMAIL-1; EMAIL-1 replied to EMAIL-2."

int moved = rewrite.mapOffset(text.indexOf("bob@example.com"));

Document labelled = Document.of(rewrite.text()).with(PiiAnnotator.PII,
    rewrite.mentions().stream()
        .map(mention -> new Annotation<>(mention.span(), mention)).toList());

			Types are case-sensitive. Labels uppercase ASCII letters in the type and
			share a numbering sequence when the displayed prefixes match. Custom
			types such as id and ID receive distinct labels.
			Numbering starts at one for each rewrite, so matching labels in separate
			documents do not identify the same value.

List<PiiMention> custom = List.of(
    new PiiMention(new Span(0, 1), "id", "a"),
    new PiiMention(new Span(2, 3), "ID", "b"));
String numbered = new Pseudonymizer().rewrite("a b", custom).text();
// "ID-1 ID-2"

			HmacTokenizer derives a token from the type and normalized value
			under a secret key. The same input and key produce the same token across
			documents. Use at least 32 random key bytes and keep the key separate from
			the data. Retain the key to compare tokens across runs. Tokens are linkable
			pseudonymous data and require access control.

byte[] key = new byte[32];
new SecureRandom().nextBytes(key);
HmacTokenizer tokenizer = new HmacTokenizer(key);

PiiAnnotator annotator = new PiiAnnotator(new CursorPiiExtractor());
Document first = annotator.annotate(Document.of("From jane@example.com"));
Document second = annotator.annotate(Document.of("To jane@example.com"));
String stable = tokenizer.token(PiiMention.TYPE_EMAIL, "jane@example.com");

String from = tokenizer.rewrite(first).text();   // "From " + stable
String to = tokenizer.rewrite(second).text();   // "To " + stable

PiiAuditReport reports mention counts, distinct-value counts and up to three sampled tokens per type by default. It stores types, counts and tokens, without normalized values or offsets. Custom types should be labels, not personal data, because they appear in the report. Tokens remain linkable pseudonymous data.
PiiAuditReport report = PiiAuditReport.of(first, tokenizer);
report.total(); // 1 report.counts().get(PiiMention.TYPE_EMAIL); // 1 report.distinctCounts().get(PiiMention.TYPE_EMAIL); // 1 report.samples(PiiMention.TYPE_EMAIL); // List.of(stable)

toString() produces one line per type. It doubles backslashes and escapes colons, commas, square brackets, control characters, Unicode formatting characters, line and paragraph separators, and unpaired surrogates as UTF-16 hexadecimal sequences. The types and tokens returned by accessors are unchanged.
List<PiiMention> customMentions = List.of( new PiiMention(new Span(0, 1), "custom\nid", "a")); PiiAuditReport customReport = PiiAuditReport.of(customMentions, tokenizer, 0); String line = customReport.toString();
The output is custom\u000aid: 1 mentions, 1 distinct on one line. This escaping is for plain-text reports, not HTML or JSON output encoding.
